K Berrien <nospamme@nospam.com> wrote:
> 
> And then how to I configure aboot after the install to boot the new
> installation?  Or are you suggesting the installer will configure all
> that...

Installer does configure "all that".  You standard "production"
kernel does not have any SCSI drivers built in at all.  They are
loaded from initrd and installer writes a corresponding 'aboot.conf'
so booting with '-flags 0' (or similarly set 'boot_osflags') works
without a need for an extra typing.

Does not mean that you cannot have your own custom kernel with
everything you want built-in but this is up to you for later.

If you have to use milo as a loader things are more "interesting". :-)

Here is the catch.  If you are updating kernels then /etc/aboot.conf
is NOT rewritten.  It is up to you do edit it properly.  I do not
recall now if a new initrd is created automatically.  You can always
do that, or rewrite the one which exists, with a help of 'mkinitrd'
script.

Updates to 7.2 are at
ftp://ftp2.compaq.com/pub/linux/RedHat/7.2-alpha/updates/
and the current kernel is 2.4.18-27.7.x.hp.  This time "milo variant"
is actually included for those without SRM. :-)

BTW - personally I am using 'sym53c8xx_2' driver and not an old
'ncr53c8xx'.  Edit /etc/modules.conf and recreate initrd to switch;
although keeping a copy of an old one could be a good idea until
you know that everything works.

  Michal

p.s. Until you really know what happens it is better to let installer
to do its job without interventions if only possible.


