K Berrien <nospamme@nospam.com> wrote:

> So the suggestion is to reinstall again,
> 
Why?  If you already installed the suggestion is boot what you have
and that is about it.  It is not clear though from your description,
at least to me, what you actually "installed".

>  booting off the cd using
> 
> boot dka400 -fl ""  (dka400 or my actual device name for cd)

No. Installation notes are pretty clear that the correct command
to start an installation would be

   boot dka400 -fl 0

unless you need something special, like a text installation or an
installation using a serial console and then that command would be

   boot dka400 -fl i

and then 'l' and pick up a proper tag for a line you want.

> and the installer will configure the booting process,

Indeed.  Although if a disk is "recycled" and there is an old
partition table on it then 'Disk Druid' will NOT change a partition
type and may not leave a space on the beginning of a disk for a boot
block. One sector will be usually more than enough (the whole "boot
support" will fit with a spare under 100k mark).  For a hard disk
booting used as a boot device with SRM you need "BSD type"
partitioning and a boot block.  If in doubt it is the best to "zero
out" few initial sectors of such disk.  OTOH nothing says that you
have to boot from something which contains your root file system.
This is _not_ the case.

> and I should be
> able to just reboot and not have to deal with SRM,

Until you will set up your SRM to autoboot you will have to
"deal with SRM" but a boot command will be

  b <your_boot_device_here> -fl 0

> and I'm good to go as I would
> be on an i386 machine after a RH install.

Actually if your firmware is OK then you are somewhat better. :-)

  Michal


